+# Adversarial Review — e2-1 (Playtest Protocol + Capture Tooling)
+
+**Reviewer:** bmad-review-adversarial-general (cynical pass)
+**Artifact:** uncommitted changes on branch `finlit-e2-1` (diff at `/tmp/finlit-e2-1-review/diff.patch`) + untracked new files (`game/scripts/playtest_session.gd`, `game/tests/test_playtest_session.gd`, `docs/playtest-scorecard.md`, story file)
+**Spec context:** `/tmp/finlit-e2-1-review/story.md` (story e2-1, status `review`)
+**Method:** every finding verified against the actual repo files in this worktree (street.gd, playtest_session.gd, test_playtest_session.gd, capture.gd, street.tscn, economy.gd, llm_tutor.gd, git state) and the Godot 4.7 `Time` API docs.
+
+Findings are descriptions only — no severity, priority, or ranking.
+
+---
+
+- The `boot` event captures an empty street name and a default age bracket on the exact scenario the playtest is designed to measure. `street.gd._ready()` calls `_load_or_new_game()` without `await`; on the no-save path that function reaches `await _ask_age_bracket()` and suspends, returning control to `_ready()`, which then runs `_record("boot", {street_name, bracket})` while `_state` is still `{}`. The payload logs `street_name: ""` and `bracket: "8-10"` (the `normalize_bracket` default) — precisely when `docs/playtest-script.md` instructs "Delete any prior save for a true first launch." The scorecard's own reference table promises `boot` carries `street_name, bracket`, and the scene test asserts `boot.street_name == "Test Lane"` — but the test only exercises the synchronous save-loaded path (it pre-seeds a save with a job), so this regression on the canonical first-launch path is invisible to the suite.
+
+- `first_wage` is not actually once-per-session, and the log cannot disambiguate game instances. The `seen_first_wage` guard lives on `_state`, and the leaderboard's NEW GAME button sets `_state = {}` then calls `_run_first_launch_flow()` without re-instantiating the street (so no new `session_begin` or `boot` either). A kid who taps 🔄 NEW GAME mid-session produces a *second* `first_wage` event, which silently breaks the scorecard's "First wage <60s" row (which says "the `first_wage` event") and the test's stated `events.count("first_wage") == 1` contract. The completion note's claim "first_wage recorded exactly once (seen_first_wage guard)" is only true per-game, not per-session; the only delimiter in the log is the `new_game` marker, and nothing in the scorecard or analysis guidance tells the reader to segment by it.
+
+- Nothing is committed and no PR exists, yet story Task 6.2 is checked `[x]` and both the story completion notes and `.memlog.md` assert the commit/push/PR shipped. `git log main..HEAD` is empty, `git status` shows every change uncommitted (modified) or untracked (the new files), and `gh pr list --head finlit-e2-1` returns nothing. AC9 ("Commit on finlit-e2-1, push, `gh pr create --base main` ... Decisions & rationale ... Never merge") is entirely unmet; the story's "Status: review — ready for code review ... `pr_review: true` on the PR" and the memlog's "story status review" / "implemented" lines describe a PR that does not exist.
+
+- The session-log filename contains a literal space and contradicts the logger's own header comment. `PlaytestSession.resolve_path` builds the timestamp with `Time.get_datetime_string_from_system(false, true)`; per the Godot 4.7 docs, `use_space = true` separates date and time with a space *instead of the letter T*. After `.replace(":", "-")` the path is `user://playtest/session-2026-08-05 00-54-00.jsonl` — a space in the filename. The class header documents the format as `session-<yyyy-mm-ddThh-mm-ss>.jsonl` (T separator), so the documented and actual formats disagree, and the unquoted space breaks naive shell/URL handling of the logged path.
+
+- The filename and the per-record `ts` field are in different time zones. The filename uses local time (`utc = false`), while `record()` writes `ts` from `Time.get_unix_time_from_system()`, which the Godot docs state "always uses UTC." Correlating a log's filename against its own `ts` values, or comparing two sessions filed across a DST transition or timezone change, drifts silently with no offset recorded anywhere in the log.
+
+- The entire reroll-attempt capture mechanism rests on an untested assumption. `_on_reroll_input` is meant to catch taps on the *disabled* REROLL button via `gui_input` (the rationale comment says disabled buttons never emit `pressed`), but the scene test never instantiates the lottery card — `_setup_street_scene` pre-seeds a save that already has a job, so first-launch (and thus the lottery card and its `reroll.gui_input.connect`) never runs. `_send_reroll_input` calls `_street._on_reroll_input(press)` directly, validating only the handler body. If a disabled `Button` does not in fact deliver `gui_input` in this Godot version, `reroll_attempt` is silently never recorded and the e2-3 reroll-policy "evidence" the story leans on is hollow — and nothing in the suite would catch it.
+
+- The `PlaytestChip` overlay is wedged into the centered top HUD row rather than pinned to a screen corner. `street.tscn` adds it under `Root/HUD` (an `HBoxContainer` with `alignment = 1`, i.e. centered) after `DebugButton`, so the live-width observer indicator ("🎥 12:34 · 47") renders inline with `BalanceChip`/`JobChip`/`TickChip`/Rank/Help/Debug and shifts the centered bar. The script targets a portrait phone window; adding another chip to that already-populated centered bar risks crowding or overflow on the target device, and a session-recording indicator conventionally belongs in a corner, not mixed into the gameplay HUD the kid reads.
+
+- A capture failure is invisible to the observer who most needs to see it. When `FileAccess.open` fails, `begin()` emits a `push_warning` and leaves `_file == null`; `record()` then early-returns, but `_playtest` is still non-null, so in a debug build `_playtest_chip.visible` still flips true and the chip permanently reads "🎥 0:00 · 0" with `event_count()` frozen at 0. An observer watching the chip has every reason to believe capture is live while zero events are being written; the warning goes only to stdout, which a kid-facing playtest does not surface.
+
+- `tutor_open` mislabels the mode when debug-compare is on but the LLM key is unset. `_show_tutor_panel` sets `mode = "compare"` as soon as `_debug_compare` is true, *before* checking `FinLitLlmTutor.is_available()`; with the key absent the compare branch actually renders "LLM off: scripted path is live" (no comparison occurs), yet the log records `mode: "compare"`. The scorecard advertises `tutor_open`'s mode as the evidence for the scripted-vs-LLM integrate-vs-drop question, so a playtest run in this common configuration logs a mode that did not actually happen.
+
+- The `event` payload omits `asset_name` even though it is present on the damaged-event dict and used in the same function. `_run_market_tick` renders the burst-pipe beat from `event.get("asset_name", "your building")`, but the `_record("event", {title, effect, cost})` call three lines down drops it. The script's acceptance row — "can the kid say what it cost *their* building" / "what just happened to your duplex?" — is exactly a per-building read, yet the machine timeline cannot tie a `🔧 damaged` event to the specific building it hit, defeating any post-hoc correlation between the kid's quote and the log.
+
+- Mid-session write failures are swallowed and the chip keeps lying. `FileAccess.store_line` and `flush` return no status in Godot 4.x and `record()` does not check them; `event_count()` is incremented before the write. If the disk fills, the path is revoked, or the flush errors mid-session, the chip's count keeps climbing (the observer sees "🎥 5:12 · 38") while the file is silently truncated at whatever line last succeeded. There is no integrity check, no re-open, and no surface for the failure.
+
+- The log is opened with `FileAccess.WRITE` (truncate) with no collision guard. Two playtest launches that resolve to the same default timestamp — possible within the same wall-clock second, or any time an explicit `--playtest-log <existing>` is reused — silently clobber the prior session with zero warning. The tests themselves rely on this truncation (they reuse `user://test_playtest_sessions/session.jsonl`), which is why the footgun survives: real sessions filed to a shared path are data-lost without notice.
+
+- The `capture.gd` seed fix is correct but minimal and leaves the rig's first-buy still misrepresentative. Changing `balance` from `$75` to `$2200` clears the bar for the hard-coded `buy_asset(state, "rental", ...)` (rental L1 is `$2000`, verified in `economy.gd`), and the added comment justifies the number solely against that rental price. But the game's own savings nudge points the kid at the *cheapest* asset (`cheapest_asset_price()` = business/Snack Cart at `$150`), so the rig's "03-after-buy" still frame is a $2000 duplex mock, not what a kid actually buys first. The fix makes the rig run without addressing why the still is unrepresentative — and the comment, by explaining only the price threshold, papers over that.
